WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.740
rsync is powerful because it can compare, update, and synchronize directory trees efficiently.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.960
That same power requires guardrails.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:17.820
A small source, destination, or option mistake can change many files quickly, especially in unattended automation.

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.540
Dry runs are valuable for high-impact synchronization.

5
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.120
A preview can show what would change before the script applies the change.

6
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:33.900
Dry-run output should be reviewed at the right level: enough to catch unexpected scope, not so much raw data that logs become noisy or sensitive.

7
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.140
Delete behavior should be deliberate, documented, and protected by validation.

8
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:47.320
If deletion is part of the workflow, the script should make that clear, require an approved source and destination, and avoid broad ambiguous patterns.

9
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.660
If deletion is not part of the workflow, options and behavior should reflect that.

10
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.140
Overwrite safety matters for releases, backups, reports, and configuration.

11
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:06.600
Protect last-known-good data where practical, use staging or versioned destinations when appropriate, and consider rollback or recovery before replacing production files.

12
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.740
A fast sync is not automatically a safe sync.

13
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.100
Incremental transfer should be monitored like any other state-changing operation.

14
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:20.680
Count changes, watch for unusual sizes or file counts, and report high-level outcomes.

15
00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:28.300
Unexpectedly large changes, missing expected files, or repeated no-change results can all indicate a workflow problem.

16
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.460
The defensive goal is controlled synchronization.

17
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:41.960
Validate scope, preview risky changes, guard deletion and overwrite behavior, check results, and make outcomes visible enough that operators can trust what happened.
